U = [
(2602, None, 2604, "Ælfheres;",
 "Wīglāf was called, Wēoxstān’s son, a dear linden-fighter, man of the Scylfings, kinsman of Ælfhere;",
 "There was a man called Wīglāf, the son of Wēoxstān, a well-loved shield-warrior of the Scylfing people and a kinsman of Ælfhere;",
 "‘Wīglāf wæs hāten’ is a passive, ‘Wīglāf was called’ (‘hāten’ is the past participle of ‘hātan’, ‘call, name’), and everything after it is apposition, naming him four ways: ‘Wēoxstānes sunu’ (genitive, ‘Wēoxstān’s son’), ‘lēoflīc lindwiga’ (nominative, ‘dear linden-fighter’: ‘lind’ is the linden-wood shield, ‘wiga’ a warrior), ‘lēod Scylfinga’ (‘man of the Scylfings’, a genitive plural) and ‘mǣg Ælfheres’ (‘kinsman of Ælfhere’). The poem has not named him before: after the retainers who ran to the wood at the end of the last part, this is the one who stayed. The Scylfings are the Swedish ruling house in the earlier parts (‘helm Scylfinga’ is a Swedish king), so a Scylfing living among the Geats is a man of Swedish kin; that is an inference from those lines, and nothing here says who Ælfhere was. ‘Wēoxstān’ here and ‘Wēohstān’ at 2613 look like one name, the ‘x’ standing for ‘hs’.", True),
(2604, "geseah", 2605, None,
 "saw his liege-lord under the war-mask suffer hotly.",
 "and saw his liege-lord suffering the heat beneath his helmet.",
 "The subject stays Wīglāf, and the pattern is ‘geseah’ (preterite of ‘sēon’, ‘saw’) + accusative object + infinitive: ‘saw his lord suffer’. ‘mondryhten’ is ‘man-lord’ (‘mon’ ‘man’ + ‘dryhten’ ‘lord’), the house ‘liege-lord’; it is Beowulf, whom Wīglāf addresses by name at 2663. ‘hāt’ is the adverb ‘hotly’ and ‘þrōwian’ ‘suffer, endure’. ‘heregrīman’ I take as a dative singular, ‘under the war-mask’: ‘here’ ‘army, war’ + ‘grīma’ ‘mask’, a helmet with a face-guard, so that the heat is felt through the iron. The fire is the dragon’s, from the fighting of the last parts.", False),
(2606, None, 2608, None,
 "He remembered then the favours which he before had given him, the wealthy dwelling-place of the Wǣgmundings, each of the folk-rights, as his father had possessed;",
 "Then he remembered the favours his lord had given him before, the rich homestead of the Wǣgmundings and every right of the people that went with it, just as his father had held them;",
 "‘Gemunde’ is the preterite of ‘gemunan’, ‘remember’, and takes the accusative ‘ðā āre’ (plural, ‘those favours’; ‘ār’ means ‘honour, favour, possession’). Two nouns then say what the favours were: ‘wīcstede weligne Wǣgmundinga’ (‘wīcstede’ ‘dwelling-place’, ‘weligne’ ‘wealthy’, and ‘Wǣgmundinga’, a genitive plural, ‘of the Wǣgmundings’, a family name) and ‘folcrihta gehwylc’ (‘each of the folk-rights’, ‘gehwylc’ ‘each’ with a genitive plural). ‘þē hē him ǣr forgeaf’ is ‘which he had earlier given him’, ‘hē’ the lord, ‘him’ Wīglāf. ‘swā his fæder āhte’ is ‘as his father owned’, the object left out. What ‘folcriht’ means in practice (‘folc’ ‘people’ + ‘riht’ ‘right’) is a guess: the standing and dues of a landholder. The Wǣgmundings are, I take it, Wīglāf’s own family, since the estate was his father’s; the text does not say so in these words.", False),
(2609, None, 2610, None,
 "he could not then hold back; the hand seized the shield, the yellow linden, drew the old sword;",
 "He could not hold himself back then: his hand took up the shield, the yellow linden, and drew the old sword;",
 "‘ne mihte ðā forhabban’ is ‘could not then hold back’; ‘forhabban’ has no object (‘himself’ is understood). Two verbs follow with no ‘and’, ‘gefēng’ (‘seized’, from ‘gefōn’) and ‘getēah’ (‘drew’, from ‘getēon’), and ‘hond’ is the subject of both, so the hand acts as if on its own; the poet makes the deed quick. ‘rond’ is the shield, and ‘geolwe linde’ (accusative, ‘yellow linden’) says the same again: shields were of linden wood, and ‘geolu’ ‘yellow’ probably names its colour or paint (a guess). ‘gomel swyrd’ is ‘old sword’, ‘swyrd’ a spelling of ‘sweord’. The next unit says whose it was.", False),
(2611, None, 2612, "Ōhtere[s];",
 "that was, among men, Ēanmund’s leaving, of the son of Ōhtere;",
 "That sword, as men reckoned, was what Ēanmund had left behind, the son of Ōhtere;",
 "‘þæt’ looks back to the sword. ‘lāf’ (cf. ‘leave’) is what remains of someone, an heirloom, here a dead man’s weapon handed on. ‘mid eldum’ is ‘among men’ (‘ylde’ ‘men’, dative plural), a phrase of report, ‘as men say’. ‘suna’ is the genitive singular of the u-stem ‘sunu’, agreeing with ‘Ēanmundes’: ‘of Ēanmund, of the son of Ōhtere’. The manuscript has ‘ohtere’; the final ‘-s’ is from Grundtvig’s Danish translation (Thorkelin’s transcript reads ‘Oþeres’), so Klaeber’s brackets mark a repair of the genitive. The apparatus also marks here the start of a new leaf (folio 188a). Eadgils was ‘sunu Ōhteres’ at 2393, so Ēanmund and Eadgils are brothers if the same Ōhtere is meant, which is likely but not stated.", False),
(2612, "þām", 2616, "etonisc;",
 "to whom at the fight became, to the friendless exile, Wēohstān slayer, with the edges of the sword, and to his kinsmen bore the brown-gleaming helm, the ringed byrnie, the old sword, ettinish;",
 "Wēohstān had become his slayer in that fight, cutting down the friendless exile with the edges of his sword; and he bore home to his own kinsmen the brown-gleaming helmet, the ringed byrnie and the old sword made by giants;",
 "A relative clause: ‘þām’ (dative, ‘to whom’, so Ēanmund) + ‘wearð’ (‘became’) + ‘Wēohstān bana’: ‘to whom Wēohstān became slayer’, which means he killed him. ‘wræcca(n) winelēasum’ is ‘to the friendless exile’ in apposition with ‘þām’ (‘wine’ ‘friend’ + ‘-lēas’ ‘without’; ‘wræcca’ ‘exile, outcast’); the ‘(n)’ is Ettmüller’s and Schücking’s addition, making the dative ‘wræccan’ agree. Klaeber’s italic ‘n’ in ‘Wēohstān’ marks a change: the manuscript has ‘weohstanes’ (genitive), which beside ‘bana’ would make Wēohstān the victim, so Grundtvig’s ‘Wēohstān’ is followed. ‘mēces ecgum’ is the instrumental, ‘by the sword’s edges’. ‘ætbær’ (‘bore to’) takes ‘his māgum’, ‘to his kinsmen’, which I read as Wēohstān’s. ‘brūnfāgne’ I render ‘brown-gleaming’ (‘brūn’ ‘brown, shining’ + ‘fāh’ ‘gleaming’); the word is shaky, since Thorkelin’s transcript has ‘hasufāgne’, ‘grey-gleaming’, and I call it a guess. In ‘hringde byrnan’ (‘ringed byrnie’) Rieger and Holthausen reverse the order for the metre; Klaeber keeps the manuscript’s. ‘ealdsweord etonisc’ (‘old sword, ettinish’) is the house wording: a blade so old it is called the work of giants, as at 1558.", False),
(2616, "þæt him", 2618, "fūslīc, —",
 "that Onela gave him, his kinsman’s war-garments, the ready army-gear —",
 "all of which Onela gave him, the war-gear of his own kinsman, the ready equipment of an army —",
 "‘þæt’ is a relative, ‘which’, looking back at the helmet, byrnie and sword; ‘him’ is Wēohstān and ‘Onela’ the giver (‘forgeaf’). ‘his gædelinges gūðgewǣdu’ and ‘fyrdsearo fūslīc’ are variation, the same gear named again: ‘gædelinges’ is a genitive, ‘of his kinsman’ (‘gædeling’ ‘companion, kinsman’); ‘gūðgewǣdu’ ‘war-garments’ (the house rendering); ‘fyrdsearo’ ‘army-gear’ (‘fyrd’ ‘army’ + ‘searo’ ‘equipment’); ‘fūslīc’ ‘ready, eager’. The kinsman whose gear it was is the dead Ēanmund: the next lines call him Onela’s brother’s child. So Onela hands the dead man’s arms to the man who killed him. Onela is named only here in this part. The dash is Klaeber’s and marks the poet’s aside.", False),
(2618, "nō ymbe", 2619, None,
 "not about that feud did he speak, though he had struck down his brother’s child.",
 "Onela said nothing of the feud, though Wēohstān had struck down his own brother’s son.",
 "‘nō ymbe ðā fǣhðe spræc’ is ‘did not speak about the feud’ (‘spræc’, preterite of ‘sprecan’; ‘ymbe’ takes the accusative). The subject is not written; the sentence runs on from Onela’s giving, so I take it as Onela. The concession ‘þēah ðe hē his brōðor bearn ābredwade’ turns the pronouns: ‘hē’ has to be Wēohstān, the killer, and ‘his’ Onela’s, so ‘brōðor bearn’ is Onela’s brother’s child, the Ēanmund of 2611, which would make Ōhtere Onela’s brother; the text gives this only through that chain. ‘ābredwade’ is a rare verb; the lexica give ‘to destroy, kill’ and I render ‘struck down’. A ‘fǣhðe’ is a blood-feud, the duty to avenge or settle a killing, and killing a king’s nephew would ordinarily start one; Onela’s silence is the point, and the poet gives no reason for it.", False),
(2620, None, 2622, None,
 "He [then] held the trappings for many half-years, sword and byrnie, until his son could perform noble-deed as his earlier-father;",
 "He kept that gear for many years, the sword and the byrnie, until his son was fit to do a nobleman’s part as his father before him had done;",
 "‘Hē’ is Wēohstān. The bracketed ‘[ðā]’ is an addition from Grundtvig, Ettmüller, Sievers and Holthausen; the manuscript reads ‘Hē frætwe gehēold’, without it. ‘gehēold’ is the preterite of ‘gehealdan’, ‘keep’; ‘fela missēra’ is ‘many’ + a genitive plural, ‘many half-years’ (a ‘missēre’ is a half-year, summer or winter, so years are counted in halves); ‘bill ond byrnan’ (‘sword and byrnie’) repeats ‘frætwe’ (‘trappings’). ‘oð ðæt his byre mihte’ is ‘until his son could’ (‘byre’ ‘son’, ‘mihte’ preterite of ‘magan’). ‘eorlscipe efnan’ is ‘perform noble-deed’ (‘eorlscipe’ the conduct of an ‘eorl’, a nobleman; ‘efnan’ ‘carry out’). In ‘swā his ǣrfæder’ the compound ‘ǣr-’ + ‘fæder’ is ‘earlier-father’, and it may mean the father before him or a forefather; I take the father, a guess.", True),
(2623, None, 2625, "forðweg. —",
 "gave him then among the Geats war-garments, of everything a countless number, when he from life departed, old, on the forth-way. —",
 "gave him then, there among the Geats, war-gear of every kind in countless number, when he departed this life, old and wise, upon the way forth. —",
 "The subject is still Wēohstān and ‘him’ is his son Wīglāf. ‘unrīm’ (‘un-’ + ‘rīm’ ‘number’, ‘countless number’) takes a genitive plural, ‘gūðgewǣda’ (‘of war-garments’; the apparatus notes that Ettmüller and Schücking read the nominative ‘-gewǣdu’), and ‘ǣghwæs’ is a genitive of respect, ‘in everything’, so ‘of every kind’. ‘mid Gēatum’ (‘among the Geats’) is the first place the passage shows where this family lives. ‘þā hē of ealdre gewāt’ is ‘when he went from life’ (‘ealdor’ ‘life’), and ‘frōd on forðweg’ is ‘old on the forth-way’: ‘frōd’ is old and experienced, ‘forðweg’ the way forth, a euphemism for dying. The dash is Klaeber’s and separates the family history from the moment of the fight.", False),
(2625, "Þā wæs", 2627, None,
 "Then was the first time for the young warrior that he the rush of war with his noble-lord should perform.",
 "Then came the first time for the young warrior that he had to take part in the rush of battle at the side of his lord.",
 "‘Þā wæs forma sīð’ is ‘then it was the first time’ (‘forma sīð’ nominative, ‘first time’), and ‘geongan cempan’ is a dative, ‘for the young warrior’. ‘þæt hē … fremman sceolde’ says what the first time was: ‘that he had to carry out’, ‘sceolde’ here ‘was bound to’. ‘gūðe rǣs’ is ‘rush of war’ (‘gūðe’ a genitive, ‘rǣs’ ‘rush, onslaught’ the object). ‘frēodryhtne’ is ‘noble-lord’ (‘frēo-’ ‘free, noble’ + ‘dryhten’ ‘lord’), built like ‘frēowine’ ‘noble-friend’. So the text itself says this is Wīglāf’s first battle, which makes his standing by at 2602–2605 the more striking.", False),
(2628, None, 2630, None,
 "Not melted for him the mind-spirit, nor did his kinsman’s leaving give way at the fight; that the worm found out, after they together had come to blows.",
 "His courage did not melt within him, nor did his kinsman’s heirloom fail in the fight; the dragon found that out once they had come together.",
 "‘Ne gemealt … nē … gewāc’ is two negated preterites, ‘did not melt … nor give way’ (‘gemeltan’, ‘gewīcan’); ‘him’ is dative, ‘for him’; ‘se mōdsefa’ is the ‘mind-spirit’ (‘mōd’ + ‘sefa’), the seat of courage, so to melt is to lose heart. Two emendations matter. ‘mǣges’ (Klaeber’s italic ‘es’): the manuscript has ‘mægenes’, ‘of his strength’, which would give ‘the remnant of his strength’; Ettmüller and Schücking’s ‘mǣges’ gives ‘his kinsman’s lāf’, the sword Wīglāf drew, which 2611 called Ēanmund’s, and I follow Klaeber. ‘þæt’ (italic ‘æt’): the manuscript has ‘þa’, ‘then’; Thorpe’s ‘þæt’ makes it the object of ‘onfand’ (‘found out’, ‘onfindan’). ‘syððan hīe tōgædre gegān hæfdon’ is a pluperfect, ‘after they had gone together’, ‘gegān’ here ‘come to grips’; I take ‘hīe’ as Wīglāf and the dragon.", False),
(2631, None, 2632, None,
 "Wīglāf spoke in speech-words, many right-words said to his companions — to him the mind was mournful —:",
 "Wīglāf spoke in formal words; he said many fitting things to his companions, and his heart was heavy:",
 "‘maðelode’ is the preterite of ‘maþelian’, ‘speak formally’ (the house ‘spoke in speech-words’); Klaeber spells it with ‘ð’ here and ‘þ’ elsewhere, one word. ‘fela’ takes a genitive plural, so ‘wordrihta fela’ is ‘many right-words’; ‘wordriht’ is a rare compound of ‘word’ and ‘riht’ (‘right’), and ‘words rightly said’ is a guess. ‘gesīðum’ is a dative plural, ‘to companions’ (‘gesīð’ ‘companion, retainer’); these are the men who fled to the wood (2596–2598). The dashes frame an aside, ‘him wæs sefa geōmor’, ‘to him the mind was mournful’, a dative of possession. The colon opens Wīglāf’s speech, which runs to 2660.", True),
(2633, None, 2638, "sweord.",
 "‘I remember that time, where we received mead, when we vowed to our lord in the beer-hall, who gave us these rings, that we would repay him the war-gear, if to him such need should befall, helmets and hard sword.",
 "‘I remember the time when we took mead in the hall and vowed to our lord, who gave us these rings, that we would repay him with war-gear, helmets and hard sword, if ever such need should befall him.",
 "‘geman’ is the present of ‘gemunan’ and takes the accusative ‘ðæt mǣl’ (‘that time’). ‘þǣr’ and ‘þonne’ both begin loose clauses: ‘þǣr wē medu þēgun’ (‘where/when we took mead’, ‘þēgun’ the plural preterite of ‘þicgan’, ‘receive’) and ‘þonne wē gehēton’ (‘when we vowed’, from ‘gehātan’). ‘ūssum hlāforde’ is a dative, ‘to our lord’; ‘bīorsele’ is ‘beer-hall’ (Klaeber’s ‘bīor-’ for ‘bēor’); ‘ðē’ is a relative, ‘who’. What they vowed follows in ‘þæt wē him … gyldan woldon’, ‘that we would pay him’, with ‘gif him … gelumpe’, ‘if … should befall him’ (a subjunctive of ‘gelimpan’; ‘þyslicu þearf’ ‘such a need’). In ‘gūðgeatwa’ Klaeber’s italic ‘at’ marks a change: the manuscript has ‘getawa’, apparently ‘gūð-getāwa’, which editors since Heyne make ‘-geatwa’, the usual word for ‘equipment’; the sense is unchanged. ‘helmas ond heard sweord’ spells out the gear; Holthausen prints ‘heardsweord’ as one word. A vow made over mead in the hall is what binds the men to the lord who gave them rings, and Wīglāf is quoting it back at those who broke it.", False),
(2638, "Ðē", 2640, None,
 "Because he chose us in the host for this venture of his own wishes, took thought of us for glories, and gave me these treasures,",
 "He chose us out of the host for this venture of his own free will, he had glory in mind for us, and he gave me these treasures,",
 "‘Ðē’ (the same as ‘þē’) here means ‘because’, and the sentence has no main clause: Wīglāf piles up reasons through 2646, and the thought they lead to, ‘so we owe him’, is left for the hearers to supply. ‘gecēas’ is the preterite of ‘gecēosan’, ‘chose’; ‘on herge’ is a dative, ‘in the host’; ‘sīðfate’ is ‘venture, expedition’; ‘sylfes willum’ is ‘by his own wishes’. ‘onmunde ūsic mǣrða’ is shaky: ‘onmunde’ occurs only here, and with the people in the accusative and ‘mǣrða’ (‘glories’) in the genitive I read ‘took thought of us for glories’, which is a guess; Bugge would replace the half-line ‘ond mē þās māðmas geaf’ with ‘ond mēda gehēt’, ‘and promised rewards’. ‘mē … geaf’ is ‘gave me’, ‘māðmas’ ‘treasures’: the gifts are Wīglāf’s own, which is why he can speak of them.", False),
(2641, None, 2642, "helmberend, —",
 "because he counted us good spear-warriors, bold helmet-bearers, —",
 "because he reckoned us good spearmen, bold wearers of the helmet —",
 "‘þē’ is ‘because’ again, the second reason. ‘tealde’ is the preterite of ‘tellan’, ‘reckon, count’, with a double accusative: ‘ūsic’ (‘us’) and ‘gārwīgend gōde’ (‘spear-warriors, good’; ‘gār’ ‘spear’ + ‘wīgend’ ‘fighters’). ‘hwate helmberend’ is variation: ‘hwate’ ‘bold, keen’ (the adjective, not the ‘Hwæt’ that opens the poem) and ‘helmberend’ ‘helmet-bearers’. The dash is Klaeber’s and marks a turn into a concession.", False),
(2642, "þēah", 2646, "dollīcra.",
 "though the lord for us this work of courage alone thought out to be done, the herdsman of the folk, because he of men most of glories had done, of deeds daring.",
 "even though our lord, the shepherd of his people, thought to do this work of courage alone on our behalf, because he had won the greatest share of glory among men, by deeds of daring.",
 "‘þēah ðe’ is ‘although’, and its verb is ‘āðōhte’ (preterite of ‘āðencan’, ‘think out, resolve upon’): ‘hlāford’ is the subject, ‘þis ellenweorc’ (‘this work of courage’) the object, ‘āna’ ‘alone’, ‘tō gefremmanne’ an inflected infinitive, ‘to be done’. ‘folces hyrde’ (‘herdsman of the folk’, a king’s title) repeats ‘hlāford’. ‘ūs’ is a dative, ‘for us’; the apparatus lists readings that make it ‘our lord’ (‘ūre’, ‘ūser’), after which no ‘for us’ is needed. ‘forðām’ introduces the reason: ‘manna mǣst mǣrða gefremede’ is ‘of men most of glories he had achieved’ (‘mǣst’ with partitive genitives ‘manna’ and ‘mǣrða’), and ‘dǣda dollīcra’ gives the means, ‘of deeds daring’. ‘dollīc’ can mean ‘rash’, even ‘foolhardy’, so the tone of the word, whether praise or a hint of blame, is open; I choose ‘daring’ from the context.", False),
(2646, "Nū", 2648, "gūðrinca;",
 "Now is the day come, that our liege-lord of strength has need, of good war-men;",
 "Now the day has come when our liege-lord has need of strength, of good fighting men;",
 "‘Nū is sē dæg cumen’ is a perfect made with ‘is’ + the participle ‘cumen’, ‘the day is come’. ‘þæt’ opens the clause that says what the day is: ‘ūre mandryhten’ (the house ‘liege-lord’, spelled ‘mondryhten’ at 2604) is the subject of ‘behōfað’, ‘has need of’, a verb that takes the genitive. Two genitives follow, ‘mægenes’ (‘of strength’) and ‘gōdra gūðrinca’ (‘of good war-men’; ‘gūð’ ‘war’ + ‘rinc’ ‘man’): strength is named in the abstract and then in the men who have it.", False),
(2648, "wutun", 2650, "grim!",
 "let us go to him, help the battle-leader, while it be the fire-terror, grim!",
 "Let us go to him and help our leader in battle, while the fire still rages, that grim terror!",
 "‘wutun’ (a form of ‘wuton’) is ‘let us’ and takes the infinitive ‘gongan’ (‘go’), with ‘tō’ as an adverb, ‘to him’; ‘helpan’ takes the dative ‘hildfruman’ (‘battle-leader’, ‘hild’ ‘battle’ + ‘fruma’ ‘first one, leader’). ‘þenden hyt sŷ’ is ‘while it be’, with ‘sŷ’ a subjunctive of ‘bēon’. ‘glēdegesa’ is ‘fire-terror’ (‘glēd’ ‘ember, flame’ + ‘egesa’ ‘terror’, with the ‘e’ dropped; Sievers and Holthausen print ‘-egsa’ for the metre), and ‘grim’ is the adjective. What ‘hyt’ means is disputed and I call my reading a guess: Klaeber’s ‘hyt’ is ‘it’, the fire or the plight; Kemble reads ‘hit [hāt]’, ‘while it is hot’; Thorpe and Sedgefield ‘hāt’ for ‘hyt’; Grein ‘hit’ as a noun, ‘heat’. Every reading gives the same urgency: the fire is burning now.", False),
(2650, "God", 2652, None,
 "God knows on me that to me it is much dearer that my body with my gold-giver the flame embrace.",
 "God knows, so far as I am concerned, that I would far rather the flame embrace my body beside my gold-giver.",
 "‘God wāt on mec’ is ‘God knows of me’ (‘wāt’ the present of ‘witan’; ‘mec’ the long accusative of ‘ic’); ‘on’ with the sense ‘concerning’ is my guess. Then two ‘þæt’ clauses: the first gives what God knows, ‘þæt mē is micle lēofre’ (‘that to me it is much dearer’, ‘lēofre’ the comparative); the second gives the thing preferred, ‘þæt … glēd fæðmię’, ‘that the flame embrace’, a subjunctive of ‘fæðmian’ (‘embrace, enfold’), with ‘glēd’ the subject and ‘mīnne līchaman’ (‘my body’) the object. The ‘ę’ at the end is the e caudata; I read it as the ordinary subjunctive ending. ‘mid mīnne goldgyfan’ is ‘with my gold-giver’ (‘gold’ + ‘gifa’): ‘mid’ ordinarily takes the dative, and the accusative here is an irregularity, though the sense is plain. To burn beside the lord who gave the rings is the choice the hall-vow of the last unit makes.", False),
(2653, None, 2656, "ðēodnes.",
 "It does not seem fitting to me that we bear shields back to the homeland, unless we first can fell the foe, defend the life of the Weders’ prince.",
 "It does not seem right to me that we should carry our shields home again, unless we have first been able to bring down the foe and defend the life of the prince of the Weders.",
 "‘Ne þynceð mē gerysne’ is an impersonal, ‘it does not seem to me fitting’ (‘þyncan’ + dative ‘mē’; ‘gerysne’ ‘fitting, becoming’). ‘þæt wē rondas beren’ is a subjunctive, ‘that we should bear shields’, ‘eft tō earde’ ‘back to the homeland’. ‘nemne we ǣror mægen’: ‘nemne’ is ‘unless’, ‘ǣror’ ‘sooner, first’, and ‘mægen’ is the present subjunctive plural of ‘magan’, ‘be able’, not the noun ‘mægen’ ‘might’. Two infinitives follow, ‘fāne gefyllan’ (‘fell the foe’) and ‘feorh ealgian’ (‘defend the life’) with ‘Wedra ðēodnes’ (genitive, ‘of the Weders’ prince’, Beowulf; ‘Wedra’ are the Geats). ‘fāne’ I take as an accusative of ‘fāh’, ‘hostile’, used as a noun, ‘the hostile one’, which is the dragon; the form is shorter than the expected ‘fāgne’, so that is a guess.", False),
(2656, "Ic", 2659, "sæcce;",
 "I know well that they were not old deserts, that he alone should, of the Geats’ tried company, suffer sorrow, sink at the fight;",
 "I know well that it is no due of his old deeds that he alone, out of the Geats’ tried warriors, should suffer grief and sink in the fight;",
 "‘Ic wāt geare’ is ‘I know well’. The next clause has a plural verb and no stated subject: ‘nǣron ealdgewyrht’ is ‘were not old deserts’ (‘ne’ + ‘wǣron’; ‘gewyrht’ ‘what is earned by deeds’, a plural neuter), and ‘þæt hē āna scyle … þrōwian’ says what is not deserved: ‘that he alone should suffer’. I read it as ‘it is no due of his old deeds that …’, a guess at an awkward construction. ‘Gēata duguðe’ is a partitive genitive, ‘of the Geats’ company’ (‘duguð’ the body of tried retainers), ‘gnorn’ is ‘sorrow, grief’ as the object of ‘þrōwian’ (‘suffer’), and ‘gesīgan’ is ‘sink, fall’. The point is the reverse of the flight of the retainers: the whole company should share the risk, and one man is bearing all of it.", False),
(2659, "ūrum", 2660, None,
 "To us both shall sword and helm, byrnie and battle-dress be in common.’",
 "Sword and helmet, byrnie and battle-dress shall be shared between the two of us.’",
 "‘ūrum’ is a dative plural of ‘ūre’, ‘our’, used as a pronoun, and ‘bām’ (dative of ‘bēgen’ ‘both’) stands with it: ‘to us both’. ‘sceal’ is singular for a list of four things. ‘gemǣne’ is ‘common, shared’. The manuscript is confused here: it has ‘urū’, with a ‘ð’ above the line and ‘ð·sceal·’ added in the margin, which looks like the scribe mending a slip; Thorpe and Grein read ‘unc’ (the dual, ‘to us two’), Sedgefield ‘hūru’ (‘indeed’). ‘beaduscrūd’ (‘battle-dress’; ‘beadu’ ‘battle’ + ‘scrūd’ ‘clothing’) carries Klaeber’s italic ‘ea’: the manuscript has ‘byrdu scrud’, and editors from Ettmüller on make it ‘beaduscrūd’; Aant. and Holthausen would also read ‘bord’ (‘shield’) for ‘byrne’. The picture I take is that the two men fight in one defence, what covers one covers both, so they stand or fall together; the exact picture is mine.", False),
(2661, None, 2662, None,
 "Waded then through the slaughter-reek, bore war-head to his lord in support, few words said:",
 "Then he went through the deadly smoke, carrying his helmet to the help of his lord, and said few words:",
 "‘Wōd’ is the preterite of ‘wadan’, ‘go, stride’, and ‘þone wælrēc’ is ‘the slaughter-reek’ (‘wæl’ ‘the slain, slaughter’ + ‘rēc’ ‘smoke’), the dragon’s fire-breath and its smoke. ‘wīgheafolan’ is an accusative of ‘wīgheafola’, ‘war-head’; the lexica take it as ‘helmet’, a head named for the war it is worn in, so ‘war-head’ in the literal line and ‘helmet’ in the idiomatic one. ‘frēan on fultum’ is ‘for his lord, as help’ (‘frēa’ ‘lord’, dative; ‘fultum’ ‘help’). ‘fēa worda cwæð’ is ‘few of words said’ (‘fēa’ with a genitive plural): after the long speech to the companions, this one is short.", True),
(2663, None, 2666, "gedrēosan;",
 "‘Dear Bīowulf, perform all well, as you in youth-life of yore said, that you would not allow, while you lived, glory to fall;",
 "‘Beloved Bīowulf, do all things well, as you said long ago in your youth, that you would not, while you lived, let your glory fall;",
 "‘Lēofa’ is a vocative, ‘dear’, and ‘lǣst’ an imperative of ‘lǣstan’, ‘carry out, fulfil’, with ‘eall tela’ (‘all well’, ‘tela’ an adverb). ‘swā ðū … gecwǣde’ is ‘as you said’ (preterite of ‘gecweðan’, here ‘promised’), ‘on geoguðfēore’ ‘in youth-life’ (‘geoguð’ ‘youth’ + ‘feorh’ ‘life’, dative) and ‘geāra’ ‘long ago’ (a genitive plural of ‘gēar’, ‘year’, as an adverb). ‘þæt ðū ne ālǣte … dōm gedrēosan’ is ‘that you would not let glory fall’: ‘ālǣte’ is a preterite subjunctive (the apparatus asks whether it should be present, ‘ālēte’), ‘be ðē lifigendum’ a dative absolute, ‘by you living’, so ‘while you live’, and ‘gedrēosan’ ‘fall, fail’. ‘dōm’ is ‘glory, judgment, reputation’. The vow Wīglāf quotes is not in this part of the poem.", False),
(2666, "scealt", 2668, None,
 "you must now, brave in deeds, resolute atheling, with all might defend your life; I will help you.’",
 "Now you must, brave in deed, resolute prince, defend your life with all your strength; I will stand by you.’",
 "‘scealt’ is the second singular, ‘you must’. ‘dǣdum rōf’ is ‘brave in deeds’ (‘rōf’ with a dative of respect), ‘æðeling anhȳdig’ ‘prince, resolute’ (‘anhȳdig’ ‘one-minded, firm of purpose’), both naming the man addressed. ‘ealle mægene’ is ‘with all might’; ‘ealle’ is not the usual dative ‘eallum’, and I take it as an instrumental of manner. ‘feorh ealgian’ is the same phrase as at 2655, but there it was the lord’s life; here Wīglāf asks Beowulf to defend his own. ‘ic ðē fullǣstu’ is ‘I help you’: ‘fullǣstan’ takes the dative ‘ðē’, and the present stands for the near future. It is the promise that the vow of the hall required of every man, made by one.", False),
(2669, None, 2672, "manna.",
 "After the words the worm came angry, terrible malice-guest, a second time, gleaming with fire-surges, to seek out foes, hateful men.",
 "After these words the dragon came on in anger, the dreadful, malicious visitor, for the second time, glowing with surges of fire, to seek out his enemies, the men he hated.",
 "‘Æfter ðām wordum’ is a dative, ‘after those words’. ‘wyrm yrre cwōm’ is ‘the worm came angry’ (‘yrre’ an adjective, ‘cwōm’ the preterite of ‘cuman’), and ‘atol inwitgæst’ renames it: ‘inwit’ ‘malice, guile’ + ‘gæst’ ‘guest’, so a malicious visitor, as ‘gryregiest’ was a terror-guest. ‘ōðre sīðe’ is a dative of time, ‘a second time’. ‘fȳrwylmum fāh’ is ‘fāh with fire-surges’ (‘fȳr’ ‘fire’ + ‘wylm’ ‘surge’, dative plural); ‘fāh’ has two senses in the poem, ‘gleaming, variegated’ and ‘stained’, and I take the first. ‘fīonda nīos(i)an, lāðra manna’ is an infinitive of purpose, ‘to seek out’, with the genitive ‘of foes, of hateful men’. The manuscript copies disagree (‘niosnan’ in B, ‘mosum’ in A); Kemble, Holthausen and Schücking print ‘nīosan’, Grein ‘nīosian’, and Klaeber’s ‘nīos(i)an’ keeps the longer form with the ‘i’ in parentheses.", True),
(2672, "Līgȳðum", 2674, None,
 "With flame-waves burned up the shield up to the rim, the byrnie could not to the young spear-warrior give help,",
 "The waves of flame burned the shield away up to its rim, and the byrnie could give the young spearman no help,",
 "‘Līgȳðum’ is a dative plural, ‘with flame-waves’ (‘līg’ ‘flame’ + ‘ȳð’ ‘wave’); ‘forborn’ is the preterite of ‘forbyrnan’, ‘burn up’, with ‘bord’ (‘shield’) as its subject. ‘wið rond[e]’ is the doubtful part. The final ‘-e’ is Kemble’s, making a dative where the manuscript has ‘rond’. ‘rond’ means ‘shield’ and also its rim or boss; ‘shield against shield’ gives nothing, so I take ‘rim’: the board burned up to its metal rim, a guess. ‘byrne ne meahte … gēoce gefremman’ is ‘the byrnie could not render help’ (‘gēoc’ ‘help’, dative; ‘gefremman’ ‘do, afford’), and ‘geongum gārwigan’ is a dative, ‘to the young spear-warrior’ (‘gār’ ‘spear’ + ‘wiga’ ‘warrior’), Wīglāf, as the next clause confirms. Mail turns blades and not fire.", False),
(2675, None, 2677, "forgrunden.",
 "but the young man under his kinsman’s shield went with courage, when his own was crushed by embers.",
 "but the young man went boldly in under his kinsman’s shield, since his own had been ground to ruin by the flames.",
 "‘ac’ is ‘but’, and ‘se maga geonga’ is ‘the young man’ (the weak adjective after its noun). ‘under his mǣges scyld’ is ‘under his kinsman’s shield’: the ‘mǣg’ here is Beowulf, so the text calls him Wīglāf’s kinsman, with no detail given. ‘elne geēode’ is ‘went with courage’ (the preterite of ‘geēode’, ‘went’; ‘elne’ a dative of manner). ‘þā his āgen w(æs) glēdum forgrunden’ is ‘when his own was ground to bits by embers’: ‘āgen’ ‘own’ stands for the shield, which the sentence does not repeat; ‘forgrunden’ is the past participle of ‘forgrindan’, ‘grind to pieces’; ‘glēdum’ is an instrumental. The letters in ‘w(æs)’ are restored from Grundtvig’s translation where the damaged manuscript is lost.", False),
(2677, "Þā gēn", 2680, "genȳded;",
 "Then still the war-king glories remembered, struck with main-strength with the battle-bill, so that it stood in the head, driven by malice;",
 "Then the war-king, still mindful of his glories, struck with all his main strength with his battle-sword, so that it stood fast in the head, driven home by hatred;",
 "‘Þā gēn’ is ‘then still’; ‘gūðcyning’ (‘war-king’) is Beowulf, the subject of both verbs. ‘m(ǣrða)’ is restored from Grundtvig’s translation, and ‘gemunde’ takes the genitive: ‘remembered glories’. ‘mægenstrengo’ is ‘main-strength’ (‘mægen’ ‘might’ + ‘strengo’ ‘strength’), a form whose case is open; I take it as ‘with main-strength’ going with ‘slōh’ (‘struck’). Rieger and Holthausen put the comma after ‘slōh’ instead of after ‘gemunde’, which would make ‘mægenstrengo’ a second thing remembered; Klaeber compares 235 f. and 1519 f. ‘hildebille’ is a dative, ‘with the battle-bill’ (‘hild’ ‘battle’ + ‘bill’ ‘blade’). ‘þæt hyt on heafolan stōd’ is ‘so that it stood in the head’ (‘heafola’ ‘head’; ‘hyt’ the sword), and ‘nīþe genȳded’ ‘forced by malice’ (‘nīþ’ ‘hostility’, ‘genȳdan’ ‘compel’).", False),
(2680, "Nægling", 2682, "grǣgmǣl.",
 "Nægling burst, failed at the fight, Bīowulf’s sword, old and grey-marked.",
 "Nægling burst asunder and failed in the fight, Bīowulf’s sword, old and grey-patterned.",
 "‘Nægling’ is the sword’s name, the subject of two preterites, ‘forbærst’ (‘burst to pieces’, from ‘forberstan’) and ‘geswāc’ (‘failed’, from ‘geswīcan’, which takes a dative of the one failed; ‘æt sæcce’ is ‘at the fight’). ‘sweord Bīowulfes’ names it again, and ‘gomol ond grǣgmǣl’ are two adjectives, ‘old and grey-marked’ (‘grǣg’ ‘grey’ + ‘mǣl’ ‘mark, pattern’, the patterned look of old iron). The name looks related to ‘nægl’, ‘nail’, which is a guess; the poem here gives no explanation of it.", False),
(2682, "Him", 2684, "hilde;",
 "To him that was not granted, that to him the edges of irons could help at the battle;",
 "It was not granted to him that the edges of swords should help him in battle;",
 "‘Him þæt gifeðe ne wæs’ is ‘that was not granted to him’ (‘gifeðe’ an adjective, ‘granted, allowed’). ‘þæt him īrenna ecge mihton helpan æt hilde’ is the thing not granted: ‘īrenna’ is a genitive plural of ‘īren’, ‘of irons’, used for ‘swords’, ‘ecge’ is the plural ‘edges’, ‘helpan’ takes the dative ‘him’, and ‘æt hilde’ is ‘at battle’. The poet says only that it was not granted him, without saying by whom.", False),
(2684, "wæs sīo", 2687, None,
 "the hand was too strong, he who every sword, by my report, with the stroke overtaxed, when he to the fight bore a weapon wondrously hard; it was not a whit the better for him.",
 "That hand was too strong: by what I have heard, it overtaxed every sword with its stroke, whenever he carried to the fight a weapon wondrously hard; and it was none the better for him.",
 "The grammar is awkward and the apparatus shows it: ‘sīo hond’ is feminine and ‘sē ðe’ masculine, so ‘sē’ points at the man; Thorpe and others print ‘sēo’ (feminine). Schücking, Holthausen and Chambers treat ‘wæs sīo hond tō strong’ as a parenthesis, so that ‘sē ðe’ means Beowulf and the hand is the man’s. ‘mēca gehwane’ is ‘each of swords’ (a genitive plural with the accusative ‘gehwane’), ‘mīne gefrǣge’ ‘by my hearsay’, and ‘swenge ofersōhte’ ‘overtaxed with the stroke’; ‘ofersēcan’ is ‘over-seek’, and ‘overtaxed’ is my guess at the sense. Bugge and Holthausen read ‘þone’ (‘which’) for ‘þonne’ (‘when’). ‘wund[r]um’: the manuscript has ‘wundū’, which could be ‘wounds’ (‘hard with wounds’); Thorpe’s ‘r’ gives the dative plural of ‘wundor’, ‘wondrously’. ‘næs him wihte ðē sēl’ is ‘it was not at all the better for him’ (‘ðē’ instrumental ‘by that’).", False),
(2688, None, 2692, "bānum;",
 "Then was the people-scather, for the third time, the fierce fire-dragon, mindful of feuds, he rushed at the brave one, when room paid him back, hot and battle-grim, enclosed the whole neck with bitter bones;",
 "Then for the third time the people-scather, the fierce fire-dragon, mindful of feuds, rushed on the bold man, as soon as room was given him; hot and battle-grim, he clasped the whole neck in his sharp teeth;",
 "The first clause is awkward: ‘Þā wæs’ stands before a noun and a finite verb, ‘rǣsde’, that already has its own subject. I take ‘Þā wæs … þriddan sīðe’ as an impersonal frame, ‘then it was the third time’, and translate loosely; that is a guess at the construction. ‘þēodsceaða’ is the house ‘people-scather’, ‘frēcne fȳrdraca’ ‘fierce fire-dragon’, and ‘fǣhða gemyndig’ ‘mindful of feuds’ (a genitive plural after ‘gemyndig’). ‘rǣsde on ðone rōfan’ is ‘rushed on the brave one’. ‘þā him rūm āgeald’ is ‘when room repaid him’: ‘āgieldan’ is ‘pay back’, ‘rūm’ ‘room’, and the sense ‘when he found an opening’ is a guess. ‘heals ealne ymbefēng’ is ‘clasped the whole neck’; ‘biteran bānum’ is ‘with bitter bones’, the teeth, though the weak ending of ‘biteran’ is odd beside the dative plural ‘bānum’. Thorpe, Trautmann and Holthausen read ‘ymb-’ for ‘ymbe-’.", True),
(2692, "hē", 2693, None,
 "he was made bloody with soul-gore, blood welled in waves.",
 "He was drenched in his life’s blood, and gore welled up in waves.",
 "‘hē’ is not named. The clause before had the dragon as subject, and the one bitten at the neck is Beowulf, so I take ‘hē’ as him. ‘geblōdegod wearð’ is a passive, ‘was made bloody’ (‘geblōdegod’ a past participle, ‘wearð’ ‘became’), and ‘sāwuldrīore’ a dative, ‘with soul-gore’ (‘sāwol’ ‘soul, life’ + ‘drēor’ ‘gore’): the blood in which the life is. ‘swāt ȳðum wēoll’ is ‘blood welled in waves’ (‘swāt’ is literally ‘sweat’, used of blood; ‘wēoll’ the preterite of ‘weallan’, ‘well, boil’; ‘ȳðum’ ‘waves’). The word ‘wēoll’ is the one used of Wīglāf’s heart at 2599 and of a father’s grief earlier. This part stops on the wound.", False),
]
